  "subdivision_of_building": "Part of rear garden ",
  "appeal_start_date": null,
  "cil_liability": false,
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The retrospective playground has resulted in undue harm caused to the existing lime tree and its root area that is within the footprint of the development  The subject lime tree is a Category A tree that is prominent and it offers high valuable amenity at the rear garden of Ellington Court, therefore, the irrevocable damage to this tree caused by the development with associated less permeable block paving which would be to the detriment of the tree is unjustifiable and would not be acceptable. The works are therefore be contrary to the advice contained within the NPPF (2024), policy G7 of the London Plan (2021) and policy DMD80 of the Development Management Document (2014).",
    "The retrospective development by virtue of its damage to the existing lime tree and roots would in due course give rise to falling branches that would be a significant safety hazard to children and residents accessing the playground. The benefits of having a retrospective playground at this location does not outweigh the harm of potential danger to residents and children as identified above and the works are thereby contrary to policies  CP31 of the Core Strategy, DMD8, DMD16 and DMD37 of the Development Management Document. Policy D3 of the London Plan (2021) as well as the NPPF (2024)."
  ],
